### Native station address requests — 2026-11-11

The production Tahoe `setMac` callback now copies the six-byte request into
its physical owner's command gate. A changed address is admitted only while
the same PCI epoch is quiesced, the EDMA queue is Prepared rather than Running
or isolated for key updates, and no scan, join, BSSID/AID or CCMP ownership is
live. It accepts the permanent EEPROM MAC or a valid locally administered
unicast override. `programAr9565StationMac` performs real PCU STA_ID and
BSSID-mask writes/readbacks on every permitted change; a failed/ambiguous
write quarantines that hardware epoch rather than advertising the new address.
The verified effective MAC is reported to native inventory and used by
association and AWDL receive admission. The selected override is reprogrammed
after a fresh PCI reset and the current identity is cleared on stop. Existing
idempotent requests do not disturb an active link. The independently verified
permanent EEPROM MAC is not replaced by the override.

This source integration does not authorize RF, prove Tahoe interface acceptance
or complete the remaining channel/regulatory/coexistence and AWDL paths. The
source wiring fixture and register-level fake test cover the boundary without
claiming device execution.

### Native Tahoe event completion retention — 2026-11-12

The producer-to-Tahoe transport now gives terminal provider shutdown its own
mailbox slot by discarding stale queued notifications, and a full event ring
may replace a noncritical notification with `ScanFinished` while preserving
older scan completions and FIFO order across circular wrap. Previously, a
burst of TX notifications could drop the only scan completion, leaving WCL
waiting without a corresponding native event. The WCL consumer additionally
checks a fresh physical-owner snapshot: a delayed same-PCI-epoch scan-finished
event cannot complete a newer scan while the radio reports an active dwell.
Generation-specific results still pass through the existing provider-side
`scanNext` check. C++14/C++17 sanitizer fixtures and source-wiring checks
exercise the portable policy and production call sites. These source checks
do not establish physical scan/RF readiness or Tahoe ABI runtime acceptance.

### Native scan notification identity — 2026-11-12

The internal PCI owner/consumer callback is now `WirelessOwnerV5`, replacing
`V4` without an implicit ABI compatibility fallback. `ScanFinished` carries
the original `LegacyScanBridge` generation as well as its PCI epoch. The
physical owner copies this generation from the pending completion ticket
*before* acknowledging it; the bounded mailbox keeps it attached to the
notification across ring wrap and noise eviction. The native WCL consumer
requires both fields to match its outstanding scan and independently checks
that the provider is live and physical dwell is finished. In particular, a
queued completion from scan A cannot complete a newer same-epoch scan B even
if B's dwell has already finished. All other notifications use generation
zero and are rejected if they claim a scan ticket.

The portable C++14/C++17 fixture and native source-wiring checks cover stale
generations, invalid zero identities, mailbox record retention and the V5
callback/consumer handoff. This only validates internal source contracts.
It does not certify the 25G332 IO80211 ABI, physical scanning, or Apple Wi-Fi
UI acceptance. Mailbox exhaustion and native event delivery still require
end-to-end operating-system and hardware validation.

### Saturated scan-event mailbox recovery — 2026-11-12

If a bounded native event mailbox fills entirely with scan completions, the
next newer bridge generation can replace a strictly older completion made
obsolete by the serial WCL scan owner. This fallback runs only after trying
to evict ordinary noise such as a TX wakeup, and it never removes a provider
stop or a same-generation or newer completion in the ring. Records retain
their remaining FIFO order after circular eviction. The producer still does
not receive an acknowledgement from the transport callback when all entries
are genuinely non-discardable; offline tests do not prove eventual OS event
delivery under arbitrary consumer starvation.

### Open scan acknowledgement gap — 2026-11-12

The V5 scan generation check protects stale-event identity but does not close
the acknowledgement/liveness contract. `SimpleATH::notifyWirelessScanFinished`
calls the `void` provider notification and then acknowledges the pending
`LegacyScanBridge` completion regardless of whether an observer was present,
whether the fixed-size transport mailbox admitted the event, or whether WCL
published `SCAN_DONE`. The transport can drop a full-ring event; the WCL
consumer posts the OS completion only later from its poll. The physical
bridge therefore cannot currently distinguish successful dispatch from a
lost notification. Do not count these source checks as a guarantee of OS scan
completion under mailbox starvation.

Required follow-up: define an explicit acceptance/acknowledgement contract
with stable epoch/generation identity, a bounded retry path owned by the
serialized provider and transport, and an OS event-consumer acknowledgement.
Never call back into the provider gate while holding the native mailbox lock,
and do not allow a stale acknowledgement to retire a successor generation.
Test missing observers, full terminal rings, stop/resume cancellation and OS
publication failures with an isolated native callback harness before claiming
reliable Tahoe scanning.
